---
/**
 * What this is, who it is for, and what it deliberately is not.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { sources } from "../lib/sources";

const sourceCount = Object.keys(sources).length;
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="An independent, cited index of AI capability, governance and safety. What it is, who it is for, and what it is not."
  cites={[]}
>
  <section class="section section--open">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="masthead">
        <h1>About</h1>
        <p class="masthead__meta mono">
          {sourceCount} sources
          <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
          zero client JavaScript
          <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
          every figure cited
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="doc">
        <p class="lede">
          The data on AI is fragmented. Epoch AI has the best capability figures and almost no
          policy. The OECD and the IAPP have policy and no capability data. Eurostat has adoption
          and neither. Nobody joins them, and practitioners say so plainly: the field is hard to
          navigate and there is no single overview of how the pieces relate.
        </p>

        <h2>What this is</h2>
        <p>
          An attempt at that overview, built for two readers at once. Someone who wants to
          understand what is actually happening in AI, and someone who wants current sourced
          numbers without visiting eight sites. The same page serves both by putting the figure
          first and the detail one click away.
        </p>
        <p>Four things it tries to do that the existing sites do not:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Put capability, adoption, governance and incidents on one screen.</li>
          <li>
            Set exposure against governance readiness, so the question "who is meeting this
            technology without the machinery to answer for it" has a picture.
          </li>
          <li>
            Index the law that actually applies to AI, including the large majority of it that
            never mentions AI.
          </li>
          <li>
            Attach provenance to every individual figure, rather than a bibliography at the
            bottom.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The one rule</h2>
        <p>
          Every source is declared once with its licence, attribution and known limitations. Every
          processed record carries its source identifier. Licence and attribution are resolved by
          joining that registry when the page renders, which makes attribution structural rather
          than a matter of discipline. The pipeline refuses to write data whose licence forbids
          republication; the site refuses to render it. Two checks, because data can also arrive
          hand-written.
        </p>
        <p>
          The practical consequence is that a takedown is a one-line change. Remove a source from
          the registry and everything derived from it stops being published.
        </p>

        <h2>What it is not</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Not a forecaster.</strong> Where a trend is fitted, it describes what already
            happened and is not extended past the last observation.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Not a ranking of countries.</strong> Governance readiness counts machinery,
            not quality. A jurisdiction can score low because it has deliberately chosen not to
            legislate.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Not legal advice.</strong> See the <a href="/terms/">terms</a>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Not a business.</strong> No accounts, no comments, no newsletter, no tracking,
            no revenue model, nothing to sign up for.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>How it is built</h2>
        <p>
          A Python pipeline fetches and processes each source, then a static site generator renders
          the result. Charts are drawn to SVG at build time, so the pages ship as markup with no
          chart library, no map tiles and no client JavaScript at all. Data refreshes daily, and a
          dataset is only rewritten when its records actually change, so a commit in the history
          means the data moved rather than that a timestamp did.
        </p>
        <p>
          What you can check is on the site rather than behind it. Every figure carries its source,
          its licence and the date it was retrieved. The <a href="/sources/">sources page</a> lists
          every dataset in use, what it is used for, and what it cannot tell you. The
          <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> sets out how the four indexes we code ourselves
          are scored, instrument by instrument, so a judgement you disagree with is a judgement you
          can locate and argue with.
        </p>

        <h2>Who made it</h2>
        <p>
          A personal project, built in the open, largely by an AI agent working under direction.
          That is worth stating on a site about AI. It is also the reason the checks in the
          repository are as paranoid as they are: several of them exist because something went
          wrong quietly and was only caught by reading the output rather than the code.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
